# The diagnostic path panel 153's completeness critic measured broken BEFORE the
# narrowing landed, and the golden that keeps it honest (2026-09-15).
#
# `one_tag_one_type` now admits one handle and one record with fields on a tag.
# `selfhost/handles.hero` had given the diagnostic machinery as a REASON for the
# old rule, in its own words: *"`record_by_tag` answers with the FIRST record
# carrying the tag, so a message about the second would carry the first's
# caret."* The critic built exactly that shape against the prototype and got it:
# `error[ffi_unknown_tag]` with the caret on `record Db tag sqlite3`, the HANDLE,
# whose line is correct C, and with a repair reading *"drop the field block"* —
# printed at a record that has no field block, while `DbValue`, the record that
# is actually wrong, was never named.
#
# **The repair is that the lookup is asked WHICH KIND the message is about.**
# Every clang message this file reads names `struct <tag>`, which is the record
# with FIELDS, so that is the one it now finds. The handle beside it is correct
# and is left alone.
#
# `sqlite3.h` reads `typedef struct sqlite3 sqlite3;` and nothing more, so the
# struct is opaque on purpose: a record with fields over it cannot be laid out,
# and that is what the author is told, on the author's own line.
#
# This file must NOT compile.

extern "sqlite3.h" link "sqlite3"
    record Db tag sqlite3
    record DbValue tag sqlite3 partial  #~ ffi_unknown_tag
        nBtree: i32
    function sqlite3_close(db: Db) -> i64

function main()
    print(0)
